{"archetypes":[{"name":"sentinel-state","focus_area":"correctness","weight":1,"rationale":"The new .step3-entry-plan-printed sentinel Bash block has DESIGN_TMPDIR empty/unset and BSD wc output risks the plan addresses only partially.","prompt_body":"Examine the proposed Step 3 entry Bash block in `skills/design/SKILL.md`: if the `current-design-env-$PPID.sh` source fails silently via the `[ -f ... ] &&` form and `DESIGN_TMPDIR` is left unset, `touch \"$DESIGN_TMPDIR/.step3-entry-plan-printed\"` would write to the filesystem root — verify whether any surrounding `set -u` guard or an explicit `DESIGN_TMPDIR` non-empty check prevents this, or whether the plan must add one. Confirm that `wc -c < \"$DESIGN_TMPDIR/plan.txt\" | tr -d ' '` on BSD macOS (which emits leading whitespace before counts) produces a clean integer with no residual whitespace when substituted into the `printf` bold-note template, distinct from the already-documented `wc -l` pipe. Also verify that Step 6's `cleanup-tmpdir.sh --dir \"$DESIGN_TMPDIR\"` call (and its conditional skip conditions in `skills/design/SKILL.md` Step 6) unconditionally removes the sentinel so a preserved `$DESIGN_TMPDIR` from a skipped cleanup cannot carry a stale sentinel into a manual re-attempt of the same session. Cite specific file paths and line ranges for any issues found, and follow the output-format rules from your outer wrapper exactly."},{"name":"env-registry","focus_area":"architecture","weight":1,"rationale":"LARCH_DESIGN_PLAN_SUMMARY_THRESHOLD is absent from the canonical env-var registry docs/configuration-and-permissions.md, and the Gate C threshold evaluation is prose-only with no Bash block, creating asymmetric execution vs the Step 3 entry Bash block.","prompt_body":"Verify that `LARCH_DESIGN_PLAN_SUMMARY_THRESHOLD` is added to `docs/configuration-and-permissions.md` — the canonical operator env-var reference where `LARCH_PROBE_TTL_SECONDS`, `LARCH_CODEX_EFFORT`, and `LARCH_TOKEN_RATE_PER_M` live — since the plan mentions only a CHANGELOG line, which is insufficient for operator discoverability. Check the execution asymmetry: the Step 3 entry print uses a Bash block that mechanically computes `wc -l` and compares to the env var, while the Gate C `### Presentation` section in `skills/design/references/approval-gates.md` is prose-directed with no Bash block — confirm the plan either adds a Bash block at Gate C or provides prose precise enough for consistent orchestrator-side threshold evaluation without one. Also verify whether the non-numeric validation guard (`case \"$_summary_threshold\" in (''|*[!0-9]*) ... esac`) actually appears in the committed Bash block text, since the inline code example in the plan body uses only `${LARCH_DESIGN_PLAN_SUMMARY_THRESHOLD:-120}` which handles only the empty-string case. Cite specific file paths and line ranges for any issues found, and follow the output-format rules from your outer wrapper exactly."},{"name":"dual-authority","focus_area":"architecture","weight":1,"rationale":"The plan adds MUST-level Gate C print language to SKILL.md Step 4b while approval-gates.md already owns the normative Gate C contract, creating two co-normative authorities that can drift; and the plan claims PATCH for runtime-surface files.","prompt_body":"Examine whether the plan creates a dual-authority problem: `skills/design/SKILL.md` Step 4b currently delegates to `approval-gates.md` via 'Execute the Gate C body', but the plan adds its own MUST-level print directive directly to Step 4b — check whether the re-fire-after-Other path (user picks Other on the Gate C prompt → full plan emitted → Gate C `AskUserQuestion` re-fires) is consistently described in both the new Step 4b language and the new Gate C `### Presentation` and `### Prompt` sections in `approval-gates.md`, or whether one location omits or contradicts the other, leaving the orchestrator with conflicting instructions. Verify the plan's version classification claim (PATCH, citing 'docs/scripts-only') against the authoritative rules in `.claude/skills/bump-version/SKILL.md`, given that `skills/design/SKILL.md` and `skills/design/references/approval-gates.md` fall under the `skills/` runtime surface defined in `AGENTS.md` — if the bump-version rules classify any `skills/` change as MINOR or higher, the plan's CHANGELOG entry and version bump steps need correction before implementation begins. Cite specific file paths and line ranges for any issues found, and follow the output-format rules from your outer wrapper exactly."}]}
